This note's purpose is to document the various sources used to construct the
PHOSIM model of the Palomar Hale 200" telescope using the Wynne corrector 
optics and LFC camera.

The optical layout of the system was derived from data obtained from a ZEMAX 
file for the telescope which was found at the cal Tech Palomar web site:
https://www.oir.caltech.edu/twiki_oir/bin/view/Palomar/HaleOptics
The file used is named: LFC_with_Wynne_Corrector.zmx.
This file was processed through the  ZMXtoPhosim.py program found at the 
PHOSIM distribution directory ../tools/ (Note: It had to be converted from 
Unicode to ASCII, See notes in the ZMXToPhosim.py program.) 

The resulting optics_0.txt file was modified in several  ways:

1. The surface of the mirror was specified to be aluminum with a 10 nm 
coating of aluminum oxide. (Pure aluminum exposed to air gains such a 
coating).

2. The material that make up the lenses was specified in the ZEMAX file as 
UBK7 which is a variant of BK7 with better UV transmission. The UBK7 material
 file was generated by the tools/material_generate.py program using the glassDataBase,tx file. This uses the Sellmeier 1 formula.  Using this for the UBK7 generation limited the minimum wavelength to 330 nm. Presumably UBK7 would be better. Also there is no opacity info. Should be very small anyway.

3.  Large Format Camera: https://sites.astro.caltech.edu/palomar/observer/200inchResources/lfc/lfccook.html 
This camera has 6 chips.  For the chips see:
a. https://instrumentation.obs.carnegiescience.edu/ccd/parts/ST-002a.pdf 
b. https://sites.astro.caltech.edu/palomar/observer/200inchResources/lfcspecs.html

Note: I could not find the number of readout_bits. I assumed 18 in the 
segmentation.txt file. (From 3.b ~17mbyte readout => 2 bytes/pixel)

Note. No filters were included in the ZEMAX file but the LFC does have them, 
I just don't know where (see ref to above in 3). So, probably the best way 
to implement filters is as a hack by using the last "lens" (L5 and L5E) 
which is the Dewar window to the CCD chip and to put a Sloan Digital Sky 
Survey ( SDSS ) filter coating on the front side and an AR coating on the 
back. Thus each optics_X.txt file has a different filter coating on the Dewar
window in this model.
 

Plate scale for Phosim optics_X.txt files (um/deg):
pixel size ~ .18" or 15um (see 3 and 4)
f number ~f/3.33

Approximate Plate scale for Phosim = 15*(60*60)/.18=300,000.0 um/deg

Chip Size: 2048*15um x 4096*15um = 30720um x 61440um
center at: 15.360mm,30.720mm   

Gap between chips: 15.0" (see 4) = 15"*300000(um/deg)/(60*60 ("/deg))
= 1250um = 1.25mm
 
Center of chips:
chip0: x = +15.36+(+1.25/2) =15.985mm , y= -30.72 + (-1.25/2) = -31.345mm
chip1: x = + 15.985mm, y= + 31.345mm
chip2: x = - 15.985mm, y = + 31.345mm
chip3: x = - 15.985mm, y =  - 31.345mm
chip4: x= + 15.985+1.25+15.360 = 32.595mm, y = 0.0mm
chip5: x = - 32.595mm, y = 0.0mm

Note: This places exact center of FOV in a gap!

Field size(0,1,2,3,4,5):  
x = ( (2 * 30.72) + 1.25 + (1.25/2) ) * 2 = 126.63mm
y = ( (61.44 + 1.25/2) * 2) = 124.13mm
or  x = (126630 / 300000) * 60 = 25.326'  
      y = (124130 / 300000) * 60 = 24.826'

  
F=focal length = A * 3.33 where 
A=aperture= 5156.2mm
f ratio f/3.33
=> F = 5156.2*3.33 = 17.17 m
(This is approximate )
